tment  another way to say is you could try to  essentially try to reduce people's  present bias or get at the sources of  people's present bias overall  there's different ways and we have only  started so thinking about it or  coming up with some potential  explanations but for example one thing  uh a paper that we have  on on sleep finds that when people are  napping in the afternoon which you know  some of you do also in my class  um that um reduces your present bias and  and will afford tasks and also uh  savings so that's to say perhaps sleep  perhaps alcohol consumption perhaps  other sort of factors  might affect people's present bias so if  we could sort of understand these  underlying factors and if you could sort  of tackle them  that would be another way of getting  around potentially self-control problems  causing trouble  by sort of getting uh at the root of  this particular issue if you think  that's  an issue overall there's some other  research that i find quite interesting  which is  uh sort of the idea that you can present  bias is very much like  related to attention in the sense that  people pay too much attention to the  present  compared to the future so now if you  could sort of make the future more  salient in certain ways if you could  make people think more about the future  how things will be like  they might sort of behave more patiently  so hal hirschfield has like a series of  papers  um and this hershey is the guy on the  left  the picture that you see who  used sort of like computer software to  make people look older  so on the right of the screen you see  how horseshoed like uh i don't know 40  years older or something  and so what they did is they try to show  people essentially pictures of their  older selves  and this is how you're going to look  like in 40 50 years from now  and you know then think about how things  might be like in terms of like  um your health and so on and so forth  and then get people to make savings  choices and there's some evidence at  least that's sort of increasing people's  focus and attention to the future  might actually make them uh more patient  and there's quite a bit of work people  of people  trying to do that um right now so um  just to give you a brief summary i think  that  i said already almost everything that's  to be said exponential discounting is  like a  workhorse model of modern economics it's  extremely important  and just to be clear it's helpful for  many applications and it's been a very  successful models  there's some limitations and some things  that we cannot explain so  quasi-hyperbolic discounting or some  forms of this  different discounting models might be  able to improve uh the predictability  of our models commitment devices in  particular may help  with improve outcomes but we haven't  quite figured out how to make them  actually most useful for the world  there's lots of work research going on  in this area  next week we're going to talk about risk  preferences in particular i'd like you  to read  the paper by raymond and thaler 2001. in  recitation this week there will be more  in time preferences in particular  related to uh  the next problem set um and then sort of  an introduction to risk preferences  if you uh don't remember what this  preferences are you should very much  come to recitation and get a refresher  of that  thank you very much
 

